Technical Report & Analysis

FPGA SoC Implementation – Custom Soft-Core Processor & Avalon Interconnect

1. Introduction

This report provides a technical analysis of the FPGA-based embedded system designed using Intel Quartus Prime and Platform Designer. The project focuses on implementing a soft-core processor integrated with an Avalon Memory-Mapped Interconnect, allowing efficient communication between system components.

2. System Architecture

2.1 Hardware Components

Soft-Core Processor: Nios II/e CPU configured for minimal resource usage.

Memory: Internal FPGA RAM blocks mapped to Avalon bus.

Communication Interface: JTAG UART for debugging and system interaction.

Avalon Interconnect: Used for high-speed data exchange between CPU and peripherals.

Clock & Reset: Configured using Platform Designer to ensure stable system operation.

2.2 Software Components

Firmware: Written in C, compiled using Nios II Software Build Tools (SBT).

Memory-Mapped I/O: Directly interacting with the Avalon bus for efficient data transfer.

UART Interface: Handles console output for debugging and real-time system feedback.

3. Implementation Details

3.1 FPGA Configuration

Quartus Prime: Used for compiling and generating the FPGA bitstream.

Platform Designer (Qsys): Configured Avalon bus and peripheral connections.

System Integration: Verified using signal tapping and simulation tools.

3.2 Embedded Software

C-based firmware handles initialization and core system logic.

Execution Flow: Processor fetches instructions from mapped memory.

UART Output: Outputs system status messages for verification.

4. Testing & Validation

4.1 Functional Verification

Simulation in Quartus: Ensured correct hardware behavior before deployment.

Avalon Bus Debugging: Used System Console to verify interconnect behavior.

Memory Access Tests: Verified read/write operations on internal RAM.

4.2 Performance Analysis

Processor Execution Time: Measured system clock cycles per instruction.

Bus Latency: Ensured efficient data transfer through Avalon Interconnect.

UART Throughput: Verified stable real-time communication over JTAG UART.

5. Conclusion

This project successfully demonstrates the design and implementation of a custom FPGA-based System-on-Chip (SoC), integrating a soft-core processor, memory-mapped communication, and embedded firmware. The system provides a flexible and scalable platform for embedded development using Avalon Interconnect and Nios II CPU.

6. Future Improvements

Optimization of Memory Access: Reducing latency in Avalon bus communication.

Additional Peripherals: Integration of timers, GPIOs, or SPI interfaces.

Real-Time OS Implementation: Exploring RTOS for improved task scheduling.

